Build this: a display-only card with two small Kyoto-serif text blocks in OPPOSITE CORNERS (top-left + bottom-right) that MATERIALIZE THROUGH A WEBGL MASK REVEAL — the same cloudy fbm dissolve + localized un-blur the blur-reveal card uses. The two corner blocks are drawn to one offscreen 2D-canvas texture (warm-gray monochrome ink on a putty plate) and uploaded to a fragment shader. Reveal: a purely 2D fractal noise field (domain-warped fbm, no column wipe) with a soft radial bias sweeps a feathered front across the plane so the text dissolves in as wispy tendrils blooming from center; where the front is passing, the texture is sampled with a multi-tap ring blur whose radius decays to zero behind it, so each part un-blurs into focus as it is uncovered, plus a subtle drift+grow so emerging text settles into place. A per-cycle seed offsets the noise so no two reveals dissolve the same. Progress is driven by a critically-damped SPRING toward a target (1 in/hold, 0 out; the exit spring is stiffer, so it clears quicker than it arrives); phase machine reveal -> hold -> clear -> swap-next-pair -> repeat, rotating a small pool of phrase pairs so different words appear each cycle. The whole plate leans subtly toward the cursor via one SHARED texture-space parallax offset (not per-letter), eased, pointer-only (rests still with no cursor). Framework-free WebGL1 + a rAF loop; DPR-capped, pauses offscreen / when hidden; reduced-motion renders one static resolved frame; falls back gracefully if WebGL is absent.

The complete, self-contained implementation follows, one file per block. It is framework-agnostic core logic — wire it into your own component and mount it on an element.

